  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/E14F9B">Remark <span data-tag="E14F9B">4.3.2.3</span>.</a> Consider a diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { \calC ^{0} \ar @{^{(}->}[d] \ar [r]^{F_0} &amp;  \calD \ar [d]^{p} \\ \calC \ar [r] \ar [ur]^{F} &amp;  \calD ' } \]
</div>
  <p> as in Definition <a href="/tag/F9A77E" data-tag="F9A77E">4.3.2.2</a>. If $C$ is an object of $\calC ^{0}$, then the functor $F_{C}: (\calC _{/C}^{0})^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \calD $ is automatically a $p$-colimit. To see this, we observe that $\id _{C}: C \rightarrow C$ is a final object of $\calC ^{0}_{/C}$. Consequently, the inclusion $\{  \id _ C \}  \rightarrow (\calC _{/C}^{0})$ is cofinal and we are reduced to proving that $F(\id _{C}): \Delta ^1 \rightarrow \calD $ is a colimit of its restriction to $\{ 0\} $, which is obvious. </p>
